#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# finite_field_index

#% text
# `finite_field_index(a)` gives the index of the finite field element `a`: `c0 + c1 p + c2 p^2 + ...` for the coefficients `c_i` in the polynomial representation, and the shown index in the exponential one.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `F(finite_field_index(a)) == a` for every element `a` of `F`.

#% item
# In the exponential representation the index of `x^k` is `k + 1` and the index of zero is 0, so the index of a nonzero element is one more than its discrete logarithm to the base `x` (`finite_field_log`).

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
F = finite_field(7, 2)

#% cell
finite_field_index(F([3, 4]))
#% output
#= 31

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# In the exponential representation:

#% cell
E = finite_field(7, 2, "Exponential")

#% cell
finite_field_index(E(10) * E(20))
#% output
#= 29

#% text
# Zero and one, in both representations:

#% cell
[finite_field_index(finite_field(5, 2)(0)), finite_field_index(finite_field(5, 2)(1)), finite_field_index(finite_field(7, 2, "Exponential")(0)), finite_field_index(finite_field(7, 2, "Exponential")(1))]
#% output
#= [0, 1, 0, 1]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The element with an index reads back:

#% cell
F(finite_field_index(F([5, 6]))) == F([5, 6])
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# The index is not additive: it is the digits `c0 + c1 p` that add, so `F(7) + F(9)` over `F_25` has the index 11, not 16:

#% cell
[finite_field_index(finite_field(5, 2)(7) + finite_field(5, 2)(9)), finite_field_index(finite_field(5, 2)(7)) + finite_field_index(finite_field(5, 2)(9))]
#% output
#= [11, 16]

#% text
# The argument must be a field element:

#% cell
finite_field_index(3)
#% output
#= finite_field_index(3)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `finite_field_element`, `finite_field_log`, `finite_field`
